{"entries":{"chapter-78-study-peripheral-vascular-disease|Chapter 78 Study: Peripheral Vascular Disease":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|PAD affects up to 20% of people over 70 and commonly reflects atherosclerotic narrowing from the infrarenal aorta to tibial vessels (p1209).":["u3"],"copy|Vascular claudication occurs at a relatively reproducible walking distance and subsides with rest. Spinal stenosis symptoms have more variable onset and may improve with forward leaning (p1210).":["u4"],"copy|Examine distal lesions and pulses, but assess motor/sensory function when acute or severe ischemia is suspected; foot viability is not determined by pulses alone (p1210).":["u5"],"copy|ABI is the highest ankle systolic pressure divided by the highest brachial systolic pressure. Further imaging should fit the specialist's diagnostic/intervention plan (p1210–1211).":["u6"],"copy|Smoking cessation and supervised walking are central. Typical exercise sessions last 30 minutes, repeated three times weekly, walking as far beyond pain onset as safely tolerated (p1211).":["u7"],"copy|Antiplatelet treatment and a high-potency statin address vascular risk. Cilostazol may improve claudication distance; use caution with heart failure (p1211).":["u8"],"copy|Consider claudication intervention for lifestyle limitation after an exercise trial; progression to limb loss is not inevitable (p1209–1210).":["u9"],"copy|Carotid plaque can embolize and cause stroke; maximize antiplatelet, lipid and risk-factor management, including smoking cessation (p1212–1213).":["u10"],"copy|The chapter generally favors endarterectomy over stenting in older patients; stenting has specific indications and is not routinely indicated for asymptomatic older patients (p1209–1210).":["u11"],"copy|An abdominal aortic aneurysm is usually defined on ultrasound by an infrarenal aortic diameter ≥3.0 cm or an enlargement at least 50% above the expected diameter. It can remain silent until expansion or rupture (p1214, USPSTF AAA screening recommendation).":["u12"],"copy|For an unruptured AAA, ACC/AHA recommends repair at a maximal diameter ≥5.5 cm in men or ≥5.0 cm in women. Symptoms attributable to the aneurysm or rapid growth may warrant earlier repair. Endovascular repair can have short-term benefits, with later outcomes dependent on anatomy, follow-up and reintervention (p1210).":["u13"],"copy|Endovascular repair still requires long-term imaging follow-up; medical management includes smoking cessation and control of comorbid vascular risks (p1210, p1215).":["u14"],"copy|Chronic venous insufficiency results from venous hypertension, usually incompetent valves; elevation and compression are the mainstays (p1216–1217).":["u15"],"copy|AAA does not automatically prohibit aerobic activity: the chapter encourages activity and notes no rupture-prevention benefit demonstrated for doxycycline (p1215).":["u16"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u17"],"copy|2023-Jun Q8":["u18"],"copy|2024-May Q27":["u19"],"copy|CHAPTER 78s / HAZZARD":["u20"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 78 - Peripheral Vascular Disease; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u21"],"copy|Peripheral arterial disease":["u22"],"copy|Management & drugs":["u23"],"copy|Carotid disease & aneurysm":["u24"],"copy|Venous disease & exam traps":["u25"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (2):":["u26"],"copy|Antiplatelet treatment and a high-potency statin address vascular risk. Cilostazol may improve claudication distance and is contraindicated in heart failure of any severity (p1211 and 2024 ACC/AHA PAD guideline, section 5.7).":["u8"],"copy|The following subspecialty questions cite eighth-edition pages in this chapter.":["u26"]},"maxId":26}
